## Domain / data errors

- Prefer one project-wide sealed failure model (e.g. `Outcome` / `Result`) — do not throw for expected business failures
- Map exceptions at the data boundary; expose typed errors to domain/presentation
- Do not mix kotlin `Result` and a custom `Outcome` in the same project unless already established

```kotlin
sealed interface DataError {
    data object Network : DataError
    data object NotFound : DataError
    data class Unknown(val message: String? = null) : DataError
}

sealed interface Outcome<out T> {
    data class Success<T>(val value: T) : Outcome<T>
    data class Failure(val error: DataError) : Outcome<Nothing>
}
```

- Use the project’s existing error types when present — do not invent a second parallel hierarchy

## ViewModel mapping

| Failure kind | UI handling |
|--------------|-------------|
| Recoverable / inline | State flags or error message field |
| One-shot (toast/snackbar/dialog) | `Effect` with `@StringRes` when possible |
| Hard stop / logout | Effect → navigation |
| Uncaught in intent coroutine | `handleError(throwable)` via `CoroutineExceptionHandler` (`04-mvi-presentation`) |

- Centralize unexpected failures in **`handleError`** at end of ViewModel — log + Crashlytics + clear loading / emit Effect
- Never show raw exception messages or stack traces to users
- Never put `Throwable` / raw HTTP bodies in UI State — map to typed error + `@StringRes` / flags
- Log failures with `Constants.TAG*` (see `16-logging`); omit PII/tokens
- Cancellation is not a user error — rethrow / ignore `CancellationException`

## Forbidden

- Swallowing errors in empty `catch`
- Leaking HTTP codes / DTO error bodies straight into State without mapping
- Business `try/catch` in Fragments